## 6. Example 2: Fibonacci Numbers

### Mathematical Formulation
$$F(n) = F(n - 1) + F(n - 2) \quad \text{for } n \ge 2$$
$$\text{Base Cases: } F(0) = 0, \quad F(1) = 1$$

### Recursion Tree for `fib(4)`

```
                         fib(4)
                       /        \
                 fib(3)          fib(2)
                /      \         /     \
            fib(2)    fib(1)   fib(1)  fib(0)
           /      \
        fib(1)   fib(0)
```

Notice how `fib(2)` and `fib(1)` are computed multiple times. This is why naive recursive Fibonacci has exponential time complexity.

### C++ Implementation

```cpp
#include <iostream>

// Returns the nth Fibonacci number (0-indexed: 0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8...)
int fibonacci(int n) {
    // 1. Base Cases
    if (n == 0) return 0;
    if (n == 1) return 1;

    // 2. Recursive Step (Tree Recursion)
    return fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2);
}

int main() {
    int n = 7;
    std::cout << "The " << n << "th Fibonacci number is: " << fibonacci(n) << "\n";
    // Output: 13
    return 0;
}
```

### Complexity Analysis
- **Time Complexity:** $\mathcal{O}(2^n)$ — Each call branches into two sub-calls.
- **Auxiliary Space:** $\mathcal{O}(n)$ — The maximum stack depth equals the height of the recursion tree ($n$).

