## 7. Example 3: Binary Search using Recursion

### Concept
Binary Search finds a target in a **sorted array** by repeatedly halving the search space:
- Calculate `mid = low + (high - low) / 2`
- If `arr[mid] == target`, element found!
- If `target < arr[mid]`, search the left half: `[low, mid - 1]`
- If `target > arr[mid]`, search the right half: `[mid + 1, high]`
- Base Case for failure: `low > high` (search space exhausted)

### Execution Trace
Searching for `target = 23` in `[2, 5, 8, 12, 16, 23, 38, 56, 72, 91]`:

```
Call 1: low = 0, high = 9 -> mid = 4 (arr[4] = 16)
        23 > 16 -> search right half: low = 5, high = 9

Call 2: low = 5, high = 9 -> mid = 7 (arr[7] = 56)
        23 < 56 -> search left half: low = 5, high = 6

Call 3: low = 5, high = 6 -> mid = 5 (arr[5] = 23)
        23 == 23 -> FOUND at index 5!
```

### C++ Implementation

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

// Recursive Binary Search
// Returns the index of target if found, otherwise returns -1
int recursiveBinarySearch(const std::vector<int>& arr, int low, int high, int target) {
    // Base Case 1: Search boundary is invalid (Element not present)
    if (low > high) {
        return -1;
    }

    // Calculate mid safely to prevent integer overflow
    int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;

    // Base Case 2: Target found at mid
    if (arr[mid] == target) {
        return mid;
    }

    // Recursive Case 1: Target lies in the left subarray
    if (target < arr[mid]) {
        return recursiveBinarySearch(arr, low, mid - 1, target);
    }

    // Recursive Case 2: Target lies in the right subarray
    return recursiveBinarySearch(arr, mid + 1, high, target);
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> numbers = {2, 5, 8, 12, 16, 23, 38, 56, 72, 91};
    int target = 23;

    int resultIndex = recursiveBinarySearch(numbers, 0, static_cast<int>(numbers.size()) - 1, target);

    if (resultIndex != -1) {
        std::cout << "Element " << target << " found at index: " << resultIndex << "\n";
    } else {
        std::cout << "Element " << target << " not found in array.\n";
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### Complexity Analysis
- **Time Complexity:**
  - Best Case: $\mathcal{O}(1)$ (Target is at the initial `mid`)
  - Worst / Average Case: $\mathcal{O}(\log n)$ (Search range halved every step)
- **Auxiliary Space:** $\mathcal{O}(\log n)$ — Stack depth corresponds to the number of divisions ($\log_2 n$).

